WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:03.600
Now, we briefly touched about virtual environments.

00:03.600 --> 00:07.520
They are an important way of storing the dependencies

00:07.520 --> 00:10.320
that your Python application requires,

00:10.320 --> 00:12.640
creating that virtual environment will make

00:12.640 --> 00:17.280
a self-contained folder that includes that full Python installation.

00:17.280 --> 00:21.040
And it's going to allow you to install dependencies that are specific

00:21.040 --> 00:25.040
to that application, and a specific version of Python,

00:25.040 --> 00:28.960
without the possibility of messing up your global

00:28.960 --> 00:32.320
version of Python, might not be the same version.

00:32.320 --> 00:34.720
You're not going to have the same libraries

00:34.720 --> 00:37.200
that you're using between different projects,

00:37.200 --> 00:40.880
which is why isolating an virtual environment is very important.

00:42.160 --> 00:45.760
You might see this shortened as virtual and for short.

00:47.600 --> 00:52.480
A standard that you might see is a requirements.txt file that's included

00:52.480 --> 00:57.040
with a Python project. It's a special file that we're going to use to tell

00:57.840 --> 01:01.360
just Python's package manager command line tool,

01:01.360 --> 01:06.480
which dependencies to install for this particular package.

01:06.480 --> 01:12.080
The format is simple. You just put each dependency manually on one line

01:12.080 --> 01:16.160
in this file. If you need to specify a specific version,

01:16.160 --> 01:20.960
you can do double equals and put the version after the name of the library.

01:21.920 --> 01:27.440
Or you can just install each dependency in your virtual environment using

01:27.440 --> 01:30.560
pip directly. Once you've done that,

01:30.560 --> 01:33.760
probably as you're building the application,

01:33.760 --> 01:38.400
you can see which packages are present in your virtual environment

01:38.400 --> 01:40.000
with a command called pip freeze.

01:42.640 --> 01:47.040
You can pipe that into a requirements.txt file to generate that for you

01:47.040 --> 01:52.400
automatically. Unfortunately, that also includes a lot of these sub-dependencies.

01:52.400 --> 01:59.040
I prefer if I'm working on a simple project to manage these requirements.txt

01:59.040 --> 02:02.160
files manually, but that's not important.

02:03.120 --> 02:06.640
If you get a project with a requirements.txt file,

02:06.640 --> 02:10.720
you can do a pip install dash in your virtual environment

02:10.720 --> 02:13.840
to install all of the dependency specified in this file.

